      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To investigate the association between Chlamydia pneumoniae infection and mortality from coronary heart disease, a nested case-control study was conducted for 39,242 Japanese who participated in the JACC Study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Immunoglobulin A and G titers of C. pneumoniae were measured in stored serum samples collected from 209 patients (113 men and 96 women) who subsequently died of coronary heart disease after 13-year follow-up; and 209 controls matched for gender, age (+/-5 years) and area of residence."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After adjustment for high-sensitive CRP and other cardiovascular risk factors, the multivariate odds ratio (OR) of coronary heart disease for highest vs. lowest tertiles of IgA titers was 2.69 (95%CI, 1.00-7.20), p=0.049 for women and 0.58 (0.23-1.46), p=0.25 for men (p for interaction=0.03). A similar association was observed by high (++) vs. low (-/+/-) titers of IgA: multivariate OR was 4.09 (0.86-19.4), p=0.07 for women."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Since high IgA titers indicate persistent and repeated infection, such a state is associated with an increased risk of coronary heart disease mortality for women."
        }
      ]},
